Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Euclidean Infrageometry", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Euclidean infrageometry is synthetic geometry on a graph ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["G", "TI"], "=", 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["V", "TI"], ",", 
    StyleBox["E", "TI"]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "e443002f-889d-4176-a5a6-6101e6d3f0cc"],
 " with its path metric ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["d", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "566eed27-3f73-4fea-8782-dab537536116"],
 ". The points are the vertices, and the segments, rays, lines, circles, \
balls and polygons are defined from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["d", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6fd17370-0790-4679-9d76-165cd93c3674"],
 " alone, as in Euclid. Riemannian infrageometry, in contrast, measures the \
substrate itself."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "GuideFunctionsSection",
 CellID->1866139230,ExpressionUUID->"a0ea3657-4c83-4862-93c8-ecf80cebc774"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Classical geometry has infinitesimality: a segment can be subdivided \
infinitely many times, whatever the scale of the observer. A graph has no \
infinitely small part. Infrageometry aggregates instead: an observer scale ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["r", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "57c3699b-14e2-4ec3-bd46-2d2383753a55"],
 ", the number of steps the observer inspects at once, must be given before \
an object makes sense."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"5c3bc016-134e-4731-accf-d8848773e85a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A construction on a graph is multi-valued: two vertices are joined by many \
geodesics, and so they have many midpoints. A construction therefore gives a \
family of objects, points in dimension 0 and paths in dimension 1, together \
with its vertex density ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], ":", 
    StyleBox["V", "TI"], "\[Rule]", "{", "0"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\[Ellipsis]", "}"}]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "327ea3a8-888d-4e93-888d-b42911e5e772"],
 ", where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   StyleBox["v", "TI"], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "84f6770f-943a-4aaa-b99e-7d4f25639416"],
 " is the number of its objects through the vertex ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["v", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "a60efea0-0a28-4754-a1d9-87b6047ba75a"],
 "."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"be38cde7-6467-477a-858d-93c0e8786805"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Let graphs converge to a space, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["V", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], ",", 
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], ",", 
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["\[Mu]", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1], "\[Rule]", 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["M", "TI"], ",", 
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], ",", 
    StyleBox["\[Mu]", "TI"]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "b887c290-ff3e-480f-b989-6d1e664d6bb4"],
 ", in the Gromov-Hausdorff sense for metric measure spaces, with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5663520c-c6f6-423f-8d18-174846d825a3"],
 " the rescaled path metric and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["\[Mu]", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "66e9738d-6790-4af8-aaee-f52672d241a5"],
 " the rescaled counting measure. We expect the normalized densities ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "/", 
   UnderscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
    StyleBox["v", "TI"],
    LimitsPositioning->False], 
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]], 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   StyleBox["v", "TI"], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "295923b2-44c8-460f-95dc-016bcf5c4eff"],
 " to converge weakly to the Dirac density of the corresponding classical \
object, concentrated on a point or along a curve. The classical objects are \
breadthless and the classical intersections unique; the constructions here \
are their finite-scale versions."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"71f5e333-0f7b-4b6e-a9ff-366ee5973869"],

Cell["\<\
Each notion has three symbols. The inert head, InfraSegment[p, q], is the \
construction stated before any graph; InfraMeasurement reads it on a graph. \
The finder, FindInfraSegment[g, p, q], gives one object as a vertex list, and \
with a trailing count a List of them. The predicate, InfraSegmentQ[g, walk], \
tests a vertex list.\
\>", "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"846031a5-76b0-431e-a6b0-c108e6723bea"],

Cell["\<\
The inert heads combine: InfraIntersection and InfraUnion of heads are heads \
again, and a head is an object of an InfraScene.\
\>", "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"04efd697-b92b-4338-a7ad-29c15a004be7"],

Cell["\t", "GuideDelimiter",ExpressionUUID->"b131d8c7-6f81-4da0-a4e6-7d3a1140c865"],

